\section{Problem description and context}
Body mass is a good indicator of an animal's condition, but flipper length is much easier to measure in the field. This project asks:
\emph{How strongly is flipper length related to body mass within each penguin species, and how much of the variation in body mass does flipper length explain?}
The answer shows whether a quick flipper measurement can serve as a useful hint of a penguin's mass. Because the three species differ clearly in size (Section~2), the relationship is analysed separately for each species.

\section{Dataset description}
The data were collected by Dr.\ Kristen Gorman and colleagues at Palmer Station, Antarctica, between 2007 and 2009 \cite{gorman2014}. For Adelie, Chinstrap and Gentoo penguins the researchers recorded body-size measurements: culmen (upper bill ridge) length and depth, flipper length and body mass, together with information such as sex, island and clutch. This analysis uses \emph{flipper length} (mm) and \emph{body mass} (g). Two of the 344 records contain no measurements and were excluded, leaving $N=342$ penguins (151 Adelie, 68 Chinstrap, 123 Gentoo).

