Gentoo penguins are clearly larger than the other two species (flipper length about 217~mm versus 190--196~mm, body mass about 5100~g versus 3700~g), and the species differ in the spread of their measurements, so a single pooled analysis would mix between-species size differences with the relationship we want to study.

\section{Method}
The analysis was written in Python and run in Visual Studio Code. For each species, body mass $y$ was described as a straight-line function of flipper length $x$,
\begin{equation}
  y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x ,
\end{equation}
where $\beta_1$ is the slope (change in predicted body mass per additional millimetre of flipper) and $\beta_0$ is the intercept (the value at zero flipper length, which has no biological meaning). The line was found by least squares, i.e.\ by minimising $\sum_i (y_i-\hat y_i)^2$, the squared differences between measured masses $y_i$ and the masses $\hat y_i$ predicted by the line. The strength of the relationship is given by the Pearson correlation coefficient $r$; the fraction of the variance in body mass that is predictable from flipper length is given by the coefficient of determination $R^2 = 1-\mathrm{SS}_\mathrm{res}/\mathrm{SS}_\mathrm{tot}$ (equal to $r^2$ for a single predictor); and a $p$-value tests the null hypothesis of no linear relationship ($\beta_1=0$).

