In all three species flipper length is positively related to body mass: penguins with longer flippers tend to be heavier, and every $p$-value is below $0.001$, so the relationships are very unlikely to be due to chance alone. The strength differs between species, from a moderate correlation for Adelie ($r=0.47$) to a stronger one for Chinstrap ($r=0.64$) and Gentoo ($r=0.70$). Flipper length explains about 22\,\% of the variation in body mass for Adelie, 41\,\% for Chinstrap and 49\,\% for Gentoo. Each additional millimetre of flipper corresponds to roughly 33, 35 and 55~g of extra predicted mass, respectively.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}\vspace{-0.2em}
Flipper length is a statistically significant predictor of body mass in all three species, but not a precise one. A small $p$-value only shows that a relationship is unlikely to be chance, whereas $R^2$ shows how well it predicts. Even for Gentoo about half of the variation in body mass is explained by something other than flipper length, and for Adelie almost 80\,\% is. The remaining scatter around the line is roughly 400~g for Adelie, 300~g for Chinstrap and 360~g for Gentoo (estimated as $\mathrm{SD}_y\sqrt{1-R^2}$ from Table~1), so two penguins of the same species and flipper length can differ in mass by several hundred grams.

Correlation does not imply causation: longer flippers probably do not make a penguin heavier, they more likely reflect overall body size and composition. The dataset also records other variables, such as sex, that likely contribute. A future analysis could include several variables at once, for example with a multiple regression, and compare body mass between groups with an ANOVA.

In summary, flipper length gives a quick and useful hint of a penguin's body mass, especially for Gentoo and Chinstrap penguins, but it cannot replace weighing the animal.

